<TopicPage slug="subjects/literature/intermediate/world-epics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Epics are <strong>cultures' founding poems — long narratives of heroes, gods and origins
    that encode a people's values</strong>. Reading them comparatively reveals both the shared
    deep pattern (the hero's journey appears everywhere) and the irreducible differences (what
    each culture thinks the journey is for).
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="four founding poems, one shared pattern, different purposes" widget="equationStep" config={epicCfg} />

  <h2>Oral tradition: how epics actually worked</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The oral-formulaic method (Parry & Lord):</strong> Homer was composed in performance — bakers of phrases ("swift-footed Achilles") assembled live from formula blocks. Lord's Yugoslav fieldwork proved living bards do exactly this.</li>
    <li><strong>Writing changed the epic:</strong> fixed texts allowed complexity beyond memory — the Mahabharata's philosophical digressions are impossible without manuscript culture.</li>
    <li><strong>Epics stay alive:</strong> the Odyssey gets a new English translation every few decades (Emily Wilson's 2017 was the first by a woman); the Mahabharata runs as a TV series; Gilgamesh was rediscovered in 1872 and still makes headlines (new tablet fragments).</li>
    <li><strong>The epic hero evolves:</strong> strength (Gilgamesh) → honour (Achilles) → duty (Arjuna) → conscience (Aeneas) — reading epics in sequence reads the history of ethics.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> George, The Epic of Gilgamesh (trans.); Lord, The Singer of Tales (1960); van Buitenen, The Mahabharata (trans.).</p>
</TopicPage>
